WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.547
You ask a language model for a query.

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:09.094
It writes valid SQL.

00:00:09.094 --> 00:00:12.641
It runs without an error and hands you a number.

00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:16.188
The number looks reasonable.

00:00:16.188 --> 00:00:19.734
But did that query answer the question you actually asked?

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:23.281
That is a separate question, and the fact that it ran does not answer it.

00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:26.828
A query that runs is not a query that is right.

00:00:26.828 --> 00:00:30.375
Closing the gap between a query that runs and a query that is correct is the whole job of this section.

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:34.617
The BIRD benchmark splits the problem in two.

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:38.858
On syntax, does the query compile and run, models score about ninety five percent.

00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:43.100
That part is solved.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.341
Semantics is the open problem: the same model scores anywhere from sixteen to seventy seven percent.

00:00:47.341 --> 00:00:51.583
The low end is one shot, a single blind attempt with no feedback.

00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:55.824
The high end is multi shot, where the model runs the query, reads the error, and tries again.

00:00:55.824 --> 00:01:00.066
The loop helps a lot, and it only works because SQL runs.

00:01:00.066 --> 00:01:04.307
But even multi shot tops out below correct.

00:01:04.307 --> 00:01:08.549
Running a wrong query more times cannot make it right.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:12.437
The model does one thing.

00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:16.324
It generates the query.

00:01:16.324 --> 00:01:20.212
You add three steps around it, and each one catches a different failure.

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:24.100
Precision pins what the question means, and catches the wrong reading.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.988
Execution runs the query on real data, and catches a crash or an empty result.

00:01:27.988 --> 00:01:31.875
Verification tests the result, and catches the subtle errors that slip past everything else.

00:01:31.875 --> 00:01:35.763
The model only generates; all three steps are yours.

00:01:35.763 --> 00:01:39.130
And here is the thread through all three.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.497
Every step is SQL.

00:01:42.497 --> 00:01:45.864
You can run it, test it, and build on it, and you can do none of that to an English sentence.

00:01:45.864 --> 00:01:49.230
That is why this skill matters more now, not less.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.597
The next three pages take the steps one at a time.

00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:55.964
First, precision.
